\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The actual compact #{\Spin(8)} has outer Lie-automorphism group #{S_3}}

\p{Let #{Q_8^+} be the positive quadratic form on #{\mathbb R^8}, and
let

##{G=\Spin(Q_8^+)\longrightarrow SO(8)}

be the actual vector-generated, reverse-norm-one carrier and its vector
action #{\pi}. The concrete Lie-group and covering results of
\ref{fcap-0037} and \ref{fcap-003A} make #{G} compact, connected, and simply
connected, with #{\pi} its two-sheeted universal cover. The even-word
sign-change map of \ref{fcap-0039} identifies this positive-square carrier
with the compact negative-square convention used in the classical
source, compatibly with the vector action.}

\p{Let #{\nu_+} and #{\nu_-} be the two real eight-dimensional half-spin
representations. Their complexifications have highest weights
#{\omega_4} and #{\omega_3}, so the comparison of \ref{fcap-003B} and the
exterior construction of \ref{fcap-002Y} identify them, up to chosen
intertwiners, with the two concrete complex half-spin modules. Average a
positive definite inner product over compact #{G}. In an orthonormal
basis either representation gives

##{\nu:G\longrightarrow O(8).}

Connectedness puts its image in #{SO(8)}. Since #{G} is simply connected,
the universal cover #{\pi} has a unique based lift

##{\Theta:G\longrightarrow G,\qquad \pi\circ\Theta=\nu.}

The two maps #{\Theta(xy)} and #{\Theta(x)\Theta(y)} are based lifts on
the connected space #{G\times G}, so uniqueness makes #{\Theta} a group
homomorphism.}

\p{Its derivative is nonzero because the half-spin representation has
nonzero weights. The kernel of the derivative is an ideal of the simple
Lie algebra #{\mathfrak{so}_8}, hence it is zero. Equal dimensions make
#{\Theta} a local Lie-group isomorphism. Its image is open, and compactness
makes it closed; connectedness of #{G} makes the image all of #{G}.
Thus #{\Theta} is a covering of a simply connected target by a connected
source and is a Lie-group automorphism. Let Lie-group automorphisms act on
representation classes by inverse pullback,

##{\varphi\mathbin{\cdot}[M]=[M\circ\varphi^{-1}].}

With this left-action convention, #{\Theta^{-1}} sends the vector class
to the chosen half-spin class because #{\pi\circ\Theta=\nu}.}

\p{Every Lie-group automorphism permutes the three classes. After inner
conjugation it preserves a chosen maximal torus and positive Weyl chamber;
its differential therefore induces a based #{D_4} root-datum
automorphism. The diagram calculation of \ref{fcap-003B} shows that it
permutes precisely the outer-node weights
#{\omega_1,\omega_3,\omega_4}. Inner automorphisms act trivially on
isomorphism classes, so inverse pullback defines a homomorphism

##{\operatorname{Out}_{\mathrm{Lie}}(G)\longrightarrow S_3.}

There is a second kind of actual Lie-group automorphism. Conjugation by any unit
vector in the Clifford algebra preserves even vector products and reverse
norm and has conjugation by its inverse as inverse. On the vector module
it gives orthogonal conjugation of #{\pi}; on the odd Clifford action it
interchanges #{S^+} and #{S^-}. Its permutation fixes the vector class and
swaps the two half-spin classes. Together with the lifted half-spin
automorphism, these permutations generate #{S_3}.}

\p{This permutation action has inner kernel. Suppose an automorphism
#{\varphi} fixes all three representation classes. Equivalence of
#{\pi\circ\varphi} with #{\pi} gives an intertwiner; after polar
normalization it is a matrix #{A\in O(8)}. If #{\det A=1}, lift #{A} to
#{g\in G}. After composing #{\varphi} with the inverse inner
automorphism #{\operatorname{Int}(g)}, its projection agrees with
#{\pi}. The pointwise discrepancy from the identity lies continuously in
#{\ker\pi=\{1,-1\}}, hence is identically #{1} on connected #{G}. The
remaining automorphism is the identity. If #{\det A=-1}, first compose
with unit-vector conjugation. The result is inner by the preceding case,
but unit-vector conjugation swaps the two half-spin classes, contradicting
the hypothesis. Therefore

##{\operatorname{Out}_{\mathrm{Lie}}(G)\simeq S_3.}

A suitable composition of the two constructed outer classes maps to a
three-cycle and therefore has order three in the outer group. This does
not show that the chosen half-spin lift #{\Theta} has order three, and
no representative has been normalized here to cube to the identity. No
splitting #{S_3\to\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathrm{Lie}}(G)} is asserted.}

\p{\citet{Chapter 5, Theorem 5.6, pp. 34--35}{adams1996exceptional}
proves the compact group theorem and the permutation of its three
eight-dimensional representations.
\citet{Section 20.3, pp. 312--314}{fulton2004representation}
independently describe the corresponding complex vector/half-spin
triality and root diagram. The carrier translation, universal-cover
lift, surjectivity, and kernel argument above place that comparison on
the actual FCAP Spin group.}

\p{The pinned declaration
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.D4Tripled.trialityAutomorphism}
has literal cube one on a tripled toral-closure carrier. Its source file
does not identify that carrier with the actual group #{G}. Accordingly it
is not used to claim cube one for #{\Theta} or as a formal badge for this
theorem.}
